.TH CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_ADD 3 "2026-09-26" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_event_loop_add \- register a selectable with an event loop
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_event_reg ccol_event_loop_add(ccol_event_loop loop, ccol_selectable sel,
                       ccol_event_handlers_t handlers, void *arg,
                       char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function registers sel with loop, which then dispatches the
readiness of sel through handlers for all the life of the registration,
or until a call to ccol_event_loop_remove().

You build sel exactly as ccol_select() builds one, and you can use
ccol_selectable_from_fd, ccol_selectable_from_circq,
ccol_selectable_from_dynq, and ccol_selectable_from_chan directly.

One registration covers exactly one direction, which is sel.dir. A caller
that wants both directions live on one fd at the same time (for example a
full-duplex pipe that a program reads and writes together) calls
ccol_event_loop_add() two times and gets two independent handles. A
caller that wants to change the direction of one registration over time
(for example a socket that connects, which wants write interest until the
connect finishes and read interest after that) uses one registration and
ccol_event_loop_modify(3). The function refuses a second selectable for a
direction that is already in use on the same fd, such as two read
registrations on one fd; the call then returns CCOL_EVENT_REG_INVALID,
and err_str names that cause (see RETURN VALUE).

A queue selectable and a ccol_channel selectable have no such restriction.
You can make any number of ccol_event_loop_add() calls for the same queue
and the same direction, in one ccol_event_loop or in several, and mix
them with a ccol_select() call that runs at the same time on the same
queue and the same direction. All of them are live together, and each one
gets a turn. This is the same guarantee that ccol_select() gives: any
number of threads can watch one queue at the same time.

When a message arrives while more than one such listener is registered,
the library wakes exactly one listener for each producer call and each
consumer call. After the callback of the woken listener returns, that
listener checks whether the queue is still ready for its direction, and
if it is, passes the wake to the next listener in the line, so no live
listener is passed over for ever. A ccol_select() caller that wakes and
then leaves without an action on the queue passes the wake in the same
way, and so does a registration that is removed, or whose loop is
destroyed, before it acted on its wake, so no wake is lost.

A registration whose callback moved at least one message is dispatched
again for as long as the queue stays ready for its direction: for a read
registration the queue holds fewer messages after the callback than before
it, and for a write registration it holds more. This holds for each
registration on its own, wherever it sits in the line and whatever the
other listeners of the queue do. A backlog therefore drains without another
send or receive, even when each callback receives one message, and a
listener that declines the queue never holds up one that takes it. A
registration whose callback neither receives nor sends passes the wake on,
and a wake that every listener declines stops once it has visited each of
them, so listeners that ignore a ready queue do not spin. A write
registration whose callback sends on every call is dispatched again for as
long as the queue has room, exactly as a writable fd is; remove it when it
has nothing to send.

A queue that you register this way must live longer than its registration.
ccol_circular_queue_destroy(3), ccol_dynamic_queue_destroy(3), and
ccol_channel_destroy(3) assert if a live registration, a ccol_select()
call or a ccol_select_timed() call watches the queue; without those
asserts, the waiter would stay linked and point to the mutex of the
queue, which the destroy is about to free. Call ccol_event_loop_remove()
before you destroy the queue. Destroying the loop first is fine, because
ccol_event_loop_destroy() and ccol_event_loop_remove() always unlink the
waiter of a queue before anything touches it again. Since
ccol_event_loop_remove() does not wait for a callback that it already
gave to a reactor thread, the destroy that comes after it blocks until
any such callback finishes with the queue. The page of that destroy names
the two things that a caller must not do while it waits.

on_readable, on_writable and on_error share one prototype:

    void fn(ccol_event_loop loop, ccol_event_reg reg,
            ccol_selectable *sel, void *arg);

loop is the loop that owns the registration, sel is its selectable, and
arg is the arg of this call. reg is the handle of the registration itself,
exactly the value that ccol_event_loop_add() returns for it, and a
callback can pass it to ccol_event_loop_remove(3),
ccol_event_loop_pause(3), ccol_event_loop_resume(3),
ccol_event_loop_modify(3) and ccol_event_loop_reg_generation(3). The
registration is live, and a callback of it can run, before
ccol_event_loop_add() returns: with num_reactor_threads > 1 a callback
can run on a dispatch worker while the calling thread is inside this
function, for example when the fd is already ready at the time of the
call.
reg is valid in that callback too, so a callback never needs to read
the handle from storage that the calling thread writes after this call
returns.

handlers.on_readable reports readiness only, for every type of selectable;
ccol_event_loop never does the receive itself. For an fd selectable, the
callback makes its own read(2) or recv(2) call on sel->fd. For a queue
selectable or a ccol_channel selectable, the callback makes its own
ccol_circq_try_recv_zc() or ccol_dynmq_try_recv_zc() call on sel->cq or
sel->dq. For a ccol_channel selectable that is ccol_circq_try_recv_zc() on
sel->cq, and never ccol_chan_try_recv_zc(): the callback runs on a thread
of the loop, which the channel counts as a worker, so a ccol_chan_* call
there can reach the other queue of the channel (see
ccol_selectable_from_chan(3)). In both cases another consumer can hold
the data first. That TOCTOU race is part of the design, and it is the
same contract as the write direction of ccol_select(): the receive call
of the callback can find nothing, and the callback must handle that well.

For a queue selectable, one notification does not always mean exactly one
message, because several sends can run faster than the dispatch and the
callback then sees them as one notification. A callback that receives one
message for each call drains a burst all the same, because the loop
dispatches the listeners again while the queue stays ready after a
callback that moved a message (see above). A callback can also receive in
a loop until the call returns ccol_container_empty, which drains a burst
in fewer dispatches.

handlers.on_writable runs with no payload, for both kinds of selectable.
The caller makes its own write(2) or send(2) call, or, for a queue, its
own try-send call, which does not block: ccol_circq_try_send_zc() on
sel->cq for a circular queue or a ccol_channel selectable, and
ccol_dynmq_send_zc() on sel->dq for a dynamic queue.

handlers.on_error runs only for an fd selectable, for each condition that
the direction of the registration has no other handler for. EPOLLERR and
EPOLLHUP go here whenever the direction is not also ready. For a read
registration with on_readable set to NULL, the close or the shutdown of
the end of the peer also goes here; that is EPOLLRDHUP, which an
ordinary TCP close(2) raises without EPOLLHUP.

If the direction does have its own handler, that handler wins the same
event whenever the direction is also ready. When data arrives together
with the error, a read registration with on_readable set gets
on_readable, not on_error: EPOLLIN and EPOLLRDHUP are not mutually
exclusive with EPOLLERR and EPOLLHUP, and the loop delivers the bytes
that arrived before the peer went away. For the same reason, a write
registration with on_writable set gets on_writable, since EPOLLOUT is
also not mutually exclusive with EPOLLERR and EPOLLHUP: a socket in an
error state is writable too, because a write(2) call on it returns an
error immediately and does not block. A queue selectable never makes an
error condition.

A registration with on_error set to NULL gets EPOLLERR and EPOLLHUP through
the handler of its own direction instead, as libevent and libuv deliver
them: on_readable for a read registration, on_writable for a write
registration. The read(2), recv(2), write(2) or send(2) call of that
handler then reports the condition: -1 with the error in errno, or 0 for an
end of file. Because a read or a write of a socket also clears a pending
socket error, a transient error (such as the ICMP port unreachable that a
connected UDP socket reports as ECONNREFUSED) costs one failed receive,
and the datagrams that arrive after it reach on_readable as usual. When
both directions of one fd have such a handler, one event reaches both:
on_readable first, then on_writable, one after the other on one thread,
and each sees whatever the other left of the condition.

Each of on_readable, on_writable, and on_error can be NULL. For an fd
selectable, the loop does not arm a direction that has no handler of its
own, so a registration can carry on_error alone and leave the handler of
its own direction NULL. Such an error-only registration costs no
dispatch until something goes wrong, in both directions; this includes
an error-only registration on the write direction of a healthy socket,
which is writable from the moment of its registration.

Every condition here, an error included, is level-triggered: the loop
reports it, and calls the handler, again and again until a call removes
or pauses the registration or the condition clears. A hang-up stays
true: a pipe whose writer closed, or a socket whose peer is gone, keeps
reporting it, and a read(2) of it keeps returning 0. A handler that reads
an end of file must therefore remove the registration (or pause it),
exactly as it must for any other readiness that it leaves in place.
Remove it before you close the fd; see ccol_event_loop_remove(3).

An fd event that no handler of the registration can take is dropped, and
the loop also stops watching that registration, because the hang-up behind
it can stay true and would otherwise be reported on every wait. Only a
registration with neither the handler of its direction nor on_error gets
there, for example a read registration with both on_readable and on_error
set to NULL whose peer hangs up. The registration stays registered, and a
registration for the other direction of the same fd stays watched.
ccol_event_loop_resume(3) and a successful ccol_event_loop_modify(3) arm
it again, whether the modify changes the direction or keeps it.

Unlike the other three, handlers.on_removed is not tied to a readiness
event. It runs exactly once, at the moment when the library can prove
that the caller can free arg. After ccol_event_loop_remove() it runs
asynchronously, never from inside that call, while
ccol_event_loop_destroy(3) runs every on_removed that is due from inside
the call, on the destroying thread, before it returns. The NOTES of
ccol_event_loop_remove(3) give the exact guarantee and explain why
ccol_event_loop_remove() itself cannot always give it synchronously. You
can leave on_removed NULL for a registration whose arg needs no notice
before a free. A call of ccol_event_loop_destroy(3) on the owning loop
from on_removed is fatal: on an ordinary removal on_removed runs on the
reactor thread, where a destroy of its own loop is refused, and during a
destroy the handle is already destroyed.

A callback runs on one of the background reactor threads of loop, never
on the thread that calls this function, and how long a callback can take
depends on the configuration of the loop. With the default of one reactor
thread, that one thread polls and runs the callbacks, so a callback that
blocks stalls every other registration on the loop for all that time;
keep such a callback short and give slow work to a thread of your own.
With more than one reactor thread, there is one dedicated poller and the
callbacks run on a separate dispatch pool. That is the purpose of that
configuration, so a callback can take real time without starving the
polling. A callback holds no internal lock of the loop while it runs, so
it can take an application lock of its own, and it can call
ccol_event_loop_modify(3), ccol_event_loop_pause(3) and
ccol_event_loop_remove(3). Those calls keep working while
ccol_event_loop_shutdown(3) or ccol_event_loop_destroy(3) drains the
loop, because loop stays a live handle until that drain ends. A callback
that removes every registration of its fd and then frees arg is
therefore safe during a destroy on another thread: no callback of those
registrations runs after the removals return.

With more than one reactor thread, the loop never runs the callback of one
registration at the same time as itself (see num_reactor_threads in
ccol_event_loop_create_with_mprocs(3)). A read registration and a write
registration that share one fd also never run at the same time as each
other, so a pair of callbacks that shares state across both directions of
one fd (such as one TLS connection object) needs no lock of its own for
that reason. This rule does not reach two different fds: the callbacks of
different registrations can run at the same time on different reactor
threads.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
loop      ccol_event_loop to register with.
sel       What to watch. See DESCRIPTION.
handlers  Group of callbacks. Each callback can be NULL.
arg       Opaque pointer that the loop gives to every callback of this
          registration.
err_str   Optional pointer that gets an error string on failure, and
          NULL on success.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
On success, the function gives a new registration handle.
On failure, it gives CCOL_EVENT_REG_INVALID. The call fails when loop
is CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID, a stale handle or a handle that a call
already destroyed; when sel is malformed, because its type or its dir
is not valid, its queue pointer is NULL, or its fd is negative; when
the direction is already in use on that fd; or when an allocation, or
a system call (epoll_ctl or kevent, eventfd, or the initialisation of a
mutex), failed. An fd that names a regular file or a directory is
always refused this way, because epoll(7) cannot watch such a file
(epoll_ctl(2) fails with EPERM); the FreeBSD build refuses it the same
way, although kqueue(2) could watch it, so that one program behaves
alike on both systems.

When err_str is not NULL, *err_str names the cause, with one message for
each class. A direction already in use gives a message that contains
"direction already in use", and no retry can succeed there. A failed
allocation gives one that contains "failed to allocate", and a failed
system call gives one that contains "a system call failed".
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
ccol_event_reg is an opaque VALUE handle, as ccol_event_loop itself is,
and not a pointer. Never cast it to void* or from void*, never compare it
through a pointer cast, and never treat it as an address. Compare it
against CCOL_EVENT_REG_INVALID, or use it in a truthiness check, because
CCOL_EVENT_REG_INVALID is 0. The library resolves every use of a
ccol_event_reg against the registration table of its own loop before it
dereferences anything, so it always finds a stale handle, or a handle
that a call already removed, and refuses it cleanly; it is never a
use-after-free. This is also true while another thread races a
ccol_event_loop_modify, ccol_event_loop_pause, ccol_event_loop_resume,
ccol_event_loop_remove, or ccol_event_loop_reg_generation call on the very
same registration.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
You can call this function at the same time as ccol_event_loop_remove()
and ccol_event_loop_modify(), and from inside a callback that runs on the
reactor thread.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void on_msg(ccol_event_loop loop, ccol_event_reg reg, ccol_selectable *sel,
            void *arg) {
    c_message_t msg;
    if (ccol_circq_try_recv_zc(sel->cq, &msg) != ccol_success) return;
    printf("job: %.*s\en", (int)msg.size, (char *)msg.data);
    free(msg.data);
}

ccol_circular_queue *jobs = ccol_circular_queue_create(64, NULL);
ccol_event_handlers_t handlers = { .on_readable = on_msg };
ccol_event_reg reg = ccol_event_loop_add(
    loop, ccol_selectable_from_circq(jobs, ccol_select_read),
                               handlers, NULL, NULL);
.fi
